/*  How this project's own Cufet programs are built.

        cufet build

    ★★ Running this file builds nothing. It defines `blueprint` and calls nothing, so what comes
    out is a PLAN; `cufet build` is what walks it. Run it plainly with `cufet blueprint.cufe` and
    you get no output and no compilation, which is correct rather than a bug.

    ── What is in scope, and what is not ──

    ★ The blueprint describes what CUFET builds, not what builds Cufet. The tools below are Cufet
    programs, so they belong here. The .NET solution does not: it produces the `cufet` this file
    depends on, so a step naming it would describe something that must already have happened. That
    stays `dotnet build Cufet.sln` — see CONTRIBUTING.

    ⚠ It runs `cufet` from PATH, which is the INSTALLED tool rather than this working copy. That is
    the right front door for someone who installed Cufet and cloned the repo, and it is the wrong
    instrument for checking a change you just made — for that, build a tool the long way:

        dotnet run --project src/App/Cufet.App.csproj -- build tools/shell.cufe

    ── What the graph says, and what it does not ──

    ★★ Nothing here declares that anything depends on anything. `needs` and `makes` are all there
    is, and the order falls out of them — both tools need `tools/terminal.cufe`, so editing it
    rebuilds both. Staleness is by content, so a step is skipped when every path it needs hashes to
    what it hashed last time AND everything it makes is still there. There is nothing to build
    twice and nothing to say twice.

    ⚠ A binary's name is the platform's business, which is why the suffix is COMPUTED. This is an
    ordinary Cufet program and can simply ask.
*/

Pull a book on blueprints.
    Bind series of records like (
        the text name,
        the series of text needs,
        the series of text makes,
        the series of text runs) to blueprint:

        /* Windows is the only platform that puts an extension on an executable. ★ `OS` is the one
           variable Windows sets and POSIX does not, so the absence is the answer elsewhere. */
        Define suffix as ".exe" when (the environment variable "OS" but void is "") is "Windows_NT", otherwise "".

        Define work as a series of records like (
            the text name,
            the series of text needs,
            the series of text makes,
            the series of text runs).

        /* Both tools pull `terminal`, so both list it. That is the only reason the build knows
           they share anything. */
        Insert a record with (
            the name "shell",
            the needs a series of text with ("tools/shell.cufe", "tools/terminal.cufe"),
            the makes a series of text with ("tools/shell{suffix}"),
            the runs a series of text with ("cufet", "build","tools/shell.cufe")) into work.

        Insert a record with (
            the name "repl",
            the needs a series of text with ("tools/repl.cufe", "tools/terminal.cufe"),
            the makes a series of text with ("tools/repl{suffix}"),
            the runs a series of text with ("cufet", "build","tools/repl.cufe")) into work.

        Return work.
    Done.
Done.
